WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.440
- Thanks to Deep Sentinel

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.110
for keeping LegalEagle safe and sound.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.880
Texas files an insane lawsuit

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.370
that goes straight to the Supreme Court,

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:08.780
that doesn't seek a recount,

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.950
it doesn't seek to add
votes that were discarded,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.630
but it seeks to invalidate
the entire election

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:16.500
for almost every swing state.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.580
- Our request is we want to be heard.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.090
The only place we can go
is the US Supreme Court.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.131
And so we're pleading
with the US Supreme Court.

00:00:24.131 --> 00:00:27.210
(microphone feedback)

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.793
(bright music)

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:35.020
- Hey, it's Devin from the future again.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.360
So funny story, about
an hour after I finished

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.100
recording this video for the first time,

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.340
the Supreme Court issued

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.490
a summary dismissal of the Texas lawsuit.

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.450
So this lawsuit is now dead.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.220
But there's so much that's interesting

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.660
about the Texas lawsuit
that even though we now know

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.320
the actual result, which
is that this case is dead,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.740
it's still worth going over

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.910
what was trying to be accomplished

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.370
in this particular lawsuit.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.180
So stick around until
the end of this video,

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.550
where Devin from the further future

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.780
will come back and actually explain

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.730
the court's dismissal of this lawsuit.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:09.850
A lot of people are calling
this a seven to two dismissal.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.980
It's actually not, for
reasons that I will explain.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.810
But let's actually dig into the analysis

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.400
of this particular lawsuit
with Devin from the past.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.070
And I'll come back in the future

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:23.980
to talk about the dismissal
that happened about an hour ago.

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.400
Yeah, it all makes sense in a timey wimey

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.279
Jeremy Bearimy sort of way.

00:01:28.279 --> 00:01:29.640
(eagle cries)
- Hey, LegalEagles,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.710
it's time to think like a politician,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.550
because you don't need
to think like a lawyer

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.600
to understand that the
Texas lawsuit is nonsense,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.720
and it's a political stunt
rather than a legal strategy.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.690
Texas filed a lawsuit
in the Supreme Court,

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:43.890
asking the Court to overturn
the presidential results

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.640
in Georgia, Michigan,
Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:47.910
The lawsuit seeks an injunction

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.200
preventing the electoral college

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.590
from voting on December 14th.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.820
The Texas case was filed
by its attorney general,

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.870
Ken Paxton, who has some
legal woes of his own

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:59.090
that we'll come back to later on.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:03.010
Why yes, that is a mugshot of
the Texas attorney general.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:06.280
17 other states filed an amicus
brief in support of Texas.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.500
This doesn't technically mean

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.690
that they've joined the lawsuit.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.290
Think of it as an aggrieved Trump state

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.030
just yelling, "Me, too."

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.590
It's a friend of the court brief.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.120
You're adding your authority

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.050
and your approval for the lawsuit,

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.140
but it actually doesn't mean

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.507
that you have joined the lawsuit.

00:02:19.507 --> 00:02:21.780
And it will have no impact on the outcome

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.700
of this particular lawsuit.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.010
Now, Trump won the popular vote in Texas

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.200
and the other 17 states that
have joined in this lawsuit.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.430
The four states they are attacking

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.130
gave the popular vote to Joe Biden.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.500
More specifically, urban
voters in those four states

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.530
selected Biden at a much higher rate

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.500
than now President Trump.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.810
And tellingly, the lawsuit
doesn't ask the court

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.050
to overturn any of the other
races on these ballots,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.260
many of which went for Republicans.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.330
It's laser-focused on stopping Joe Biden

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.890
from becoming president
by disenfranchising voters

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.530
in urban areas and their suburbs.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.370
22 states and Washington DC have now filed

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.400
their own amicus briefs in
opposition to the Texas suit.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.450
Now, before I delve into these claims,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.970
here's a quick reminder about
how American elections work,

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.520
because it's crazy system,
but it's our system,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.010
and it's important to
understand this lawsuit.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:09.560
The Constitution's election clause,

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.900
article one, section four,
empowers states to determine

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.810
the time, places, and manner
of congressional elections.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.450
Subject, of course, to
Congress's authority

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.980
to make or alter state regulations.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:20.640
State and local governments
have the authority

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.900
to pass election laws,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.860
including rules about voter registration,

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:25.740
voter protection, fraud prevention,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.520
vote counting, and determination
of election results.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.870
Now, America does not
have national elections,

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.690
as we are very cognizant of.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.930
The 50 states run their
individual elections

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:36.790
as they see fit.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.400
And as long as those rules
don't violate the Constitution,

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:40.840
the states can do what they want.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.150
And generally, Republicans
have been in favor

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.750
of this patchwork approach

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.660
because it allows them the flexibility

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:48.510
in certain states, generally the red ones,

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.300
to put in generally more
restrictive voting laws,

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.950
often claiming it's necessary
to prevent voter fraud.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.500
And conservatives once
applauded this design

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.320
as an example of federalism
and states' rights,

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:01.660
because it gives states the
right to run their own affairs.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.620
Remember when Republicans used
to care about states' rights?

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.650
The Texas lawsuit wants the Supreme Court

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:08.900
to turn against federalism and nationalize

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.640
the rules for voting effectively.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.180
You can argue that this
would be a good thing

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.500
and that our system doesn't make sense.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:15.980
I would probably agree with you.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.480
But changing it isn't something

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.500
the Supreme Court can generally do,

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.010
especially after the election
has already occurred.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:24.690
And you'd have to change the Constitution

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:26.880
to require uniformity in voting laws.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.930
It would require a
constitutional amendment.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.620
Of course, both Congress
and state governments

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:34.410
are constrained by the Constitution
as it currently exists.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:35.550
But with that out of the way,

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.080
let's turn to the Texas lawsuit,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.750
and start at the beginning and ask,

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:41.290
does this case satisfy
the threshold requirements

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.270
for Supreme Court jurisdiction?

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:44.410
Now, I'll warn you ahead of time.

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:47.210
We're going to get into
the incredibly sexy topic

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.190
of Supreme Court jurisdiction.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:52.150
So if there are any young
children watching this video,

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:55.100
please ask them to leave the
room or avert their attention,

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.060
because it's gonna get
a little out of hand.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:58.893
But that being said,

00:04:58.893 --> 00:05:00.470
Texas alleges that the Biden states

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:01.910
made unconstitutional decisions

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.970
about how they decided
to run the 2020 election.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:07.340
But before we dig into
the merits of this claim,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.800
Texas has to prove that
the Supreme Court can

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.950
and should hear this particular case,

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:14.740
because it's very difficult
to get into the Supreme Court.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:17.616
And as we've often talked
about on this channel,

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:20.440
almost any time someone says
that they're going to take this

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.410
all the way to the Supreme
Court, it's total nonsense,

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.580
because there's a long,
long way you have to go

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:27.000
before you can get a case
to the Supreme Court.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.260
But Texas has skipped all of that

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:30.790
and they went straight
to the Supreme Court.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:32.680
And it's not as crazy as it first seems,

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:35.290
because article three, section
two of the Constitution

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.820
establishes the Supreme
Court's jurisdiction.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.030
The court has original
jurisdiction over lawsuits

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.050
between two or more states.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:43.510
The court has appellate jurisdiction

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.310
on almost any other case that involves

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.470
a point of constitutional or federal law.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.240
But Texas is invoking the
Court's original jurisdiction,

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:51.790
which means that this is a new case

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:54.330
that can bypass all of
the other lower courts.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:57.544
But this doesn't mean that the
Court has to hear the case.

00:05:57.544 --> 00:05:59.630
The state has to file a motion for leave

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.370
asking the Court to hear the dispute.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:02.800
And that's what Texas did.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.530
However, Texas went one step further

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.820
by applying for emergency
injunctive relief.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:08.670
Texas says that the Court
should intervene quickly

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:11.180
and stop the four defendant
states from relying

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:13.980
on the popular vote results
and appointing electors.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:15.250
In other words, Texas would like

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:16.903
the Supreme Court to
tell those four states

00:06:16.903 --> 00:06:19.110
that they need to cancel the Biden votes.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:21.660
And then they just simply
want the small favor

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.950
that the Court will force the legislatures

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.750
in those four states to appoint electors

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.330
who will select Trump,

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.620
ignoring what the majority of voters

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.980
in that state wanted and
what state law demands,

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:33.896
you know, no big deal.

00:06:33.896 --> 00:06:37.510
Now, when the court accepts
original jurisdiction cases

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:39.410
with facts disputes, it usually appoints

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:41.150
a special master to gather evidence.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:43.410
And this process usually
takes a long time.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.620
State disputes over water
rights, for instance,

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:48.460
have lingered in the
Supreme Court for 20 years.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.460
But President Trump doesn't have 20 years.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.640
So the Paxton Texas brief
argues that the Supreme Court

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:56.070
should hurry up and decide this
with no additional briefing.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:58.200
That's why Texas argues that the issues

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.420
in this litigation are neither
fact-bound nor complex.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:03.430
But this does bring up
another obvious question.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:06.710
If the issues are obvious and non-complex,

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:08.520
why did Texas wait until a month

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.600
after the election to file the lawsuit?

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:13.440
And why haven't any of the
other lawsuits succeeded?

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.620
Now, the Supreme Court's
original jurisdiction

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:17.240
can get complex, but generally speaking,

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:18.880
the Supreme Court has narrowly interpreted

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.230
its constitutional grant
of original jurisdiction.

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:22.930
Whether the Supreme Court has jurisdiction

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:24.050
is a threshold question,

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:26.100
which means that the
Court has to be satisfied

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.280
it should take this case up
before getting to the merits.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.370
And there are many threshold
problems with this lawsuit.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.170
The first of which is
the question of standing,

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:37.030
which is a topic that
we've covered many times

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:39.950
in relation to the election lawsuits.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:41.540
The first problem is that Texas

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:43.610
may not have standing to bring the case.

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.130
Standing is the ability of a party

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:45.963
to demonstrate to the court

00:07:45.963 --> 00:07:48.190
that it has enough connections
to the alleged harm

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:50.170
that it has the right to file a lawsuit.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:51.548
Generally speaking, to file a lawsuit,

00:07:51.548 --> 00:07:54.190
you have to be the party
that was actually harmed.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:56.410
For example, you can't go
into court and say that, well,

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:57.650
my neighbor's house was robbed

00:07:57.650 --> 00:07:59.350
and I wanna file suit on their behalf.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:00.311
You don't get to do that.

00:08:00.311 --> 00:08:03.130
You have to actually
personally have been harmed.

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.640
And here, the right party
to challenge election laws

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:06.810
in other states would probably be

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:09.880
the candidates and voters of that state.

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:13.800
It's a little crazy for a state
to say that they were harmed

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.860
by what happened in
another state's election.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.250
But President Trump and
the other candidates,

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.490
and the voters have
already had the opportunity

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:24.240
to litigate their claims in those states.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.990
But generally speaking, they've
lost all of those cases.

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:29.070
Texas argues that it has standing

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:30.940
because what the other states do

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:33.450
impacts who won the presidential election.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:34.520
But if that's the case,

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.690
then other states should
started suing Texas

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.300
because they disagree with
the state's election rules.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:40.860
It would basically just destroy

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.210
the entire concept of standing.

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:46.507
And as Texas GOP Whip John Thune confirms,

00:08:46.507 --> 00:08:48.300
"I just don't know why a state like Texas,

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.800
which never wants anybody
telling them what to do

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.390
now wants to tell a bunch of other states

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:54.394
how to run their elections."

00:08:54.394 --> 00:08:57.143
Yeah, it's kind of crazy.

00:08:57.143 --> 00:09:00.090
And ding, ding, ding,
ding, ding from the future.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:02.490
That's exactly how the Court came down,

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:05.960
saying that Texas has absolutely
no standing whatsoever

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:08.460
to be filing this lawsuit
in the first place.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.110
Got that right.
(eagle cries)

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.670
But that takes us to the
second problem, estoppel.

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:14.830
The second problem is
that these same claims

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:18.170
have already been
adjudicated in other courts.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:19.950
There's a legal doctrine called estoppel

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:21.300
that prevents the parties from litigating

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.250
the same cause of action or issues

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.080
once they've already been
decided by other courts.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:28.430
It's sort of like double
jeopardy, but for civil cases.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:30.400
Now, Republicans have tried
to get the Supreme Court

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.830
to hear the cases that the
lower courts have dismissed.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:34.580
But so far, the Court has declined.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.890
And here, Texas is simply
trying to do an end run

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:40.400
around those other cases,
which they already lost.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:41.970
Which brings us to the third problem,

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:43.670
which is the doctrine of laches,

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:45.380
which means a party is barred

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.240
if it unreasonably takes too long

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:49.000
before bringing a legal claim.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:51.900
And here, Texas complains
about changes to election laws

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.801
in other states that happened
months before the elections.

00:09:54.801 --> 00:09:57.290
The alleged issues Texas complains about

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:00.260
were also obvious while the
voters were still being counted.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.470
And under federal law, election disputes

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:04.580
are supposed to be resolved by six days

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:06.730
before the electoral college meets,

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:10.280
a deadline known as the safe
harbor, which was December 8th.

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:12.820
The Texas lawsuit wasn't
filed until December 7th,

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:14.070
a day before.

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:16.070
Why didn't Texas do it sooner?

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:19.190
And why didn't Texas
file before the election,

00:10:19.190 --> 00:10:22.140
if it already knew what the problems were

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:23.260
in the other states?

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:25.670
And as one of the briefs make clear,

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:26.740
it's because they didn't know

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.380
who was going to win the election,

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:32.850
which is the epitome of writing
the quiet part out loud.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:36.440
I mean, writing the
quiet part in the brief.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:39.870
It's just crass and unbelievable.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.040
Which takes us to the fourth problem,

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.500
which is known as the
political question doctrine.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.670
And to be the kind of matter
that the Court can adjudicate,

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:48.530
the dispute needs to be a
legal one, not a political one.

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:50.230
And when the Court
decides whether the issue

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:51.820
is a political one or a legal one,

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:52.920
it looks to whether the problem

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.410
is within the purview of
another branch of government.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:56.830
In this case, the Constitution provides

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:58.610
that the Congress is the ultimate judge

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:00.260
of the electoral college results.

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:01.530
Republicans are well aware of this,

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.010
because they are already
plotting a floor fight

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:06.870
when Congress meets to
certify the results.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:10.260
So that kind of seems
like a political question.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:11.350
Which takes us to the fifth problem,

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.870
which is the case is arguably moot.

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:15.765
A dispute can become
moot when the controversy

00:11:15.765 --> 00:11:18.400
initially existed at the
time the lawsuit was filed,

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:20.930
and it's no longer live
due to a change in the law

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:22.530
or status of the parties involved.

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:25.300
And 49 of 50 states have already certified

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.860
their election results
by the safe harbor day.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:31.400
And the president and his
team of Kraken wranglers

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:32.560
have already been litigating

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:34.540
these issues since election day.

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:38.150
And they've lost basically
every single one of these cases.

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:39.850
They've lost over 60 times.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:41.680
And arguably, there's nothing left

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:43.740
for the Supreme Court to decide.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:44.830
But you know what issue isn't moot

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:46.500
is hitting the notification bell.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.710
I was checking my stats
and only 10% of people

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:50.410
have enabled notifications.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:51.320
It'd mean the world to me

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:53.900
if you would subscribe and
hit that notification bell,

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:55.720
so you'll know when a video comes out.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:57.080
Which takes us to the sixth problem,

00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:59.070
which is that the remedy that Texas wants

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:01.120
to disenfranchise millions of voters

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:02.420
after they've already voted

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:05.640
is probably grossly unconstitutional.

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:06.830
Texas wants the Supreme Court

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.240
to extend the electoral college deadline.

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:10.870
The Supreme Court has never granted

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:12.880
such an extreme remedy in a voting case.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.290
It didn't even do this
in Bush versus Gore.

00:12:15.290 --> 00:12:18.730
That case involved 537 votes in one state.

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:22.670
- While I strongly disagree
with the Court's decision,

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:24.070
I accept it.

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:26.510
- And this is radically different

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:27.940
considering what's at stake here.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:30.410
But Texas thinks that the
Republican legislators

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:32.780
in the four defendant
states should be allowed

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:35.950
to cancel all the votes
and just pick Trump.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:37.480
In our system, the people have a right

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:38.940
to cast votes for the president.

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:41.470
The states in question don't
like who the voters selected.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:44.740
So they want the Republican
legislatures to pick instead.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:46.380
That's extraordinary and also

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:49.310
incredibly dangerous to democracy itself.

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:51.340
But all right, let me get
down from my soap box,

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:53.040
and let's play along with the lawsuit.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:54.550
Let's say that the Supreme Court believes

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:57.440
this suit satisfies all of
these threshold questions,

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:59.390
and then it turns to the merits.

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:01.290
Well, we get into a lot
of the same questions

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:02.320
that we've already talked about

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:03.890
in terms of election lawsuits.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:05.910
Texas alleges that the
four defendant states

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.100
violated their own laws

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:10.160
by changing their election
procedures in 2020.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:12.450
The theory is that this violated
the equal protection clause

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:13.870
by allowing different sets of voters

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:16.670
to be treated differently
within their states.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:19.150
Even if this were a valid
equal protection claim,

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:21.320
it would not overturn the
electoral college results,

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:23.440
because the votes have already been cast.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.160
The remedy would not be to
disallow millions of ballots

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:28.400
that were cast by voters
who did nothing wrong.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.660
All these voters did was follow the rules.

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:34.730
Now, Texas and 17 other states
want those votes canceled.

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:37.820
But how would that be
equitable or constitutional?

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:39.730
And we already talked about how bogus

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:40.980
these equal protection claims are

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:43.300
in the context of the Kelly
versus Pennsylvania suit

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:45.320
in a prior election video.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:47.340
But disenfranchising one set of voters

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.420
would present its own
equal protection problems,

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:51.010
especially since the president is obsessed

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:53.510
with what happened in urban areas,

00:13:53.510 --> 00:13:57.070
which voted overwhelmingly
for future President Biden.

00:13:57.070 --> 00:14:00.010
Texas is arguing that
those votes don't matter.

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:02.910
And AG Paxton is already
on very thin ice here,

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:04.730
because he's already raised all of these

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:06.610
fake election fraud claims
in the lower courts,

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:08.790
which have already been dismissed.

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:10.250
Texas claims that there are quote,

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:12.180
voting irregularities
that would be consistent

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.360
with the unconstitutional
relaxation of ballot

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:17.130
integrity protections in
those states' election laws.

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:18.780
And there's a long list of complaints

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:19.930
that we've already heard about.

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:21.170
And generally, which has already

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:22.700
been adjudicated in other places.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:24.290
Allegedly missing USB drives,

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.330
ballots being delivered in food trucks,

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:27.990
alleged statistical disparities,

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:29.470
poll workers doing something nefarious

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:31.460
when other poll watchers weren't looking.

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:33.490
These are allegations that
have already been addressed

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:35.180
in many different jurisdictions.

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:36.830
Almost always, the court finds them

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:39.020
to be completely bogus and made up.

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:41.250
And even if all of those
allegations were true,

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:42.340
which time and time again,

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:44.080
it's been proved that they aren't,

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.940
why would Texas have any
kind of standing whatsoever

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:50.110
to make a claim about those allegations?

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:51.420
It doesn't make any sense.

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:53.230
And that brings us to the electors clause.

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.330
Texas also claims that
it's unconstitutional

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:57.340
for a state legislature
to delegate their power

00:14:57.340 --> 00:14:59.500
to make election laws to state courts

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:00.900
or even to the governor.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:02.250
Texas claims that by quote,

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.910
taking or allowing non-legislative actions

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:06.290
to change the election results

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:09.320
that would govern the appointment
of presidential electors,

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:11.710
the defendant states have
violated the electors clause,

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:13.920
the equal protection clause,
and the due process clause.

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:16.840
And ironically, it turns
out that Texas is guilty

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:19.510
of doing the very thing
that it complains about.

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:22.730
Texas Governor Greg Abbott
extended the early voting period.

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:25.070
Is Texas suing itself?

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:27.300
Spoiler alert, it is not.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:30.400
But Texas is supported in
these claims by 17 states

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:33.050
who filed the friend of the
court brief, an amicus brief.

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:35.210
These states argue that
it was unconstitutional

00:15:35.210 --> 00:15:38.480
for Pennsylvania judges to let
absentee ballots be counted

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:39.990
if they were postmarked by election day,

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:41.270
but arrived afterward.

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:43.650
Like Texas, these states are utterly blind

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:45.060
to their own hypocrisy.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:47.790
Kansas and Mississippi
also count mail-in ballots

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:49.030
if they arrive late.

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:50.650
So are they calling out their own laws

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:52.220
as unconstitutional as well?

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:53.850
It doesn't really seem like it.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:56.410
But again, the fact that
states have different laws

00:15:56.410 --> 00:15:59.060
about voting doesn't violate
the elections clause.

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:01.410
The Constitution was set up that way.

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:03.130
The elections clause give states the right

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:06.160
to decide the way votes
are cast and counted.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:09.610
It doesn't give Texas or any
other state a right to veto.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:12.780
Okay, but enough about
the merits or lack thereof

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:14.170
of this particular lawsuit.

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:16.550
Let's talk about some of
the procedural high jinks.

00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:19.380
First, there is President
Trump's motion to intervene.

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:22.230
President Trump filed a motion
to intervene in this lawsuit.

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:25.420
Remember, this was brought by
the Texas attorney general.

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:27.210
Here, the president, for some reason,

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:28.950
is being represented by John Eastman,

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:31.270
a lawyer who is most famous
for writing an article

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:34.680
suggesting that as an
American-born child of immigrants,

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.693
Kamala Harris wasn't
really an American citizen.

00:16:37.693 --> 00:16:40.530
It sort of goes downhill from there.

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:43.110
But what is President
Trump actually arguing?

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.830
Well, quote, despite the
chaos of the election night

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:47.130
and the days which followed,

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:48.730
the media has consistently proclaimed

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:50.920
that no widespread voter
fraud has been proven.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:52.900
But this observation misses the point.

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:53.870
The constitutional issue

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:56.070
is not whether voters committed fraud,

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:58.210
but whether state
officials violated the law

00:16:58.210 --> 00:16:59.460
by systematically loosening

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:01.120
the measure for ballot integrity

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.640
so that fraud becomes undetectable.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:05.460
They're arguing that the fraud is so good

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:09.390
that it can't be detected,
therefore, President Trump wins.

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:11.990
I don't need to tell you
that this is pure nonsense

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:13.370
that contradicts what the Trump lawyers

00:17:13.370 --> 00:17:14.670
have been saying for over a month,

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:17.270
that poll watcher saw
the stuff that was fraud,

00:17:17.270 --> 00:17:19.840
and that the Dominion voting
machines created fraud,

00:17:19.840 --> 00:17:21.500
and that the Democrats injected

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:23.790
fake ballots into the final counts.

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:26.240
Every lawsuit filed by the Kraken says

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:29.260
that she figured out the entire scheme.

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:31.900
But now Eastman argues that quote,

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:33.490
it is not necessary for the plaintiffs

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:35.550
in interventions to prove
that fraud occurred.

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:37.700
It's only necessary to
demonstrate that the elections

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:39.890
in the defendant states
materially deviated

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:41.690
from the manner of choosing electors

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:44.390
established by their
respective state legislatures.

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:45.990
Now, I will point out parenthetically

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:49.000
that perhaps the biggest irony
of this motion to intervene,

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:50.980
where the president
says that he would like

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:53.146
to be a party in this lawsuit actually

00:17:53.146 --> 00:17:57.080
may prevent the court from
actually addressing the lawsuit.

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:59.130
Because remember, we talked
about original jurisdiction.

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:01.080
And the fact that Trump is arguing

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:03.082
he has standing to be in this lawsuit,

00:18:03.082 --> 00:18:06.390
sort of cast doubt on the
idea that this is a lawsuit

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:08.610
between one state and the other states.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:12.210
It shows that this case can
and should have been litigated

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:15.230
in the various states where
the harm allegedly occurred

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:17.190
and doesn't need original jurisdiction

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:18.260
before the Supreme Court.

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:19.130
It's the ultimate irony

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:21.830
that by blustering his way into the suit,

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:24.490
he may actually be making it
jurisdictionally impossible

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:26.970
for the Supreme Court to
actually adjudicate it.

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:28.920
Now, you might be saying, Devin,

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:30.330
are these states really suing

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:32.410
because they don't like the outcome?

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:35.740
Well, yes, it seems that's
exactly what they're saying.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:39.390
This lawsuit is very possibly
nothing but bad faith.

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:41.150
Now, keep in mind that Pennsylvania

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:42.700
has been fighting the same issues

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:43.730
since before the election,

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:45.780
and the courts keep ruling
in the state's favor,

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:48.580
even when the judges are Trump appointees.

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:50.310
But in this particular case,

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:53.800
and I usually don't engage
in this kind of speculation,

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:56.320
it's possible that the
attorney general for Texas

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:57.910
might have brought the suit

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:00.860
for reasons unrelated
to the election itself,

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:03.710
but actually to try and curry
favor with the president

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:06.070
to get a presidential pardon.

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:08.350
As ridiculous as that sounds,

00:19:08.350 --> 00:19:10.510
that seems to be the most likely reason

00:19:10.510 --> 00:19:12.310
for filing this suit.

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:15.190
Texas attorney general Paxton
has been under indictment

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:17.490
since 2015 on securities fraud charges

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:20.700
relating to activities that
happened before he was elected.

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:22.760
These generally are state charges.

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.170
And just for the hell of it,

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:26.600
here's Ken Paxton stealing $1,000 pen

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:28.810
from the same courthouse
where he was indicted.

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:30.670
- [Narrator] This is Ken Paxton,

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:32.870
the indicted Texas attorney general,

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:35.430
rummaging through the metal detector trays

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:38.490
and stealing that $1,000 pen.

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:42.780
And this is Ken Paxton, the
indicted Texas attorney general,

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:45.330
walking away with a stolen pen.

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:47.210
- But then, earlier this year,

00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:50.310
he was accused of committing
crimes while in office.

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:52.710
This time, the call came
from inside the House.

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:55.670
His own aides contacted federal
law enforcement authorities

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:57.710
and asked them to investigate allegations

00:19:57.710 --> 00:19:59.980
of improper influence, abuse of office,

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:02.130
bribery, and other potential crimes.

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:04.220
Several lawyers resigned,

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:06.530
another personnel say that
they're being retaliated against

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:08.760
for reporting Paxton's criminality.

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:10.220
The Texas solicitor general

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:12.520
who would normally join a filing like this

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:15.060
in the Texas versus Pennsylvania lawsuit

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:16.930
did not sign any of these pleadings,

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:18.460
which indicates that he doesn't want

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:21.120
to be associated with this lawsuit.

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:22.800
And he'd probably like
to avoid being swept up

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:24.800
in Paxton's issues himself.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:26.800
Now, why is all of this relevant?

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:31.214
Well, Ken Paxton could actually
use a presidential pardon.

00:20:31.214 --> 00:20:33.210
He hasn't been charged
with a federal crime yet,

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:36.030
but just this week, the
FBI subpoenaed his office,

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.370
indicating that an
investigation is ongoing.

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:40.990
Paxton chatted on the
phone with President Trump,

00:20:40.990 --> 00:20:42.920
and then he filed this lawsuit.

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:44.300
But don't take my word for it.

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:46.280
Even Republican Senator Ben Sasse

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:48.540
sees this as a pardon grab.

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:50.620
Now in passing, I'll mention
a couple of the other

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:51.840
amicus briefs that were filed.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:54.490
These are not binding, they're
not parties to the suit,

00:20:54.490 --> 00:20:56.740
but it's interesting that they
filed these with the court.

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:58.900
They want their opinions to be known.

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:00.470
And Montana Governor Steve Bullock

00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:01.860
filed his own amicus brief

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:04.250
to make it clear that
neither Texas nor Trump

00:21:04.250 --> 00:21:07.100
sought to throw out the selection
of presidential electors

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:10.260
in states that made the same
legal changes to voting rules

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:12.710
as the defendant states, stating quote,

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:15.050
Texas chose not to include
the state of Montana

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:16.750
where President Trump
and other Republicans

00:21:16.750 --> 00:21:19.270
were successful in a mail ballot election

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.610
conducted to reduce the impact of COVID.

00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:23.790
Underscoring, of course, that this action

00:21:23.790 --> 00:21:25.880
is less about election
integrity than it is

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.720
about attempting to overturn
the will of the electorate.

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:30.980
But if Texas is successful in its suit,

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:33.280
it would destabilize the
results of elections in Montana

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:35.890
and any other state that
took valid state law actions

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:38.370
to minimize the impact
of the virus on voting,

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:40.130
including states that delivered victories

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:42.740
to Republican candidates
using mail ballots.

00:21:42.740 --> 00:21:44.820
And then of course, there
were the insane ramblings

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:46.210
of the amicus brief on behalf

00:21:46.210 --> 00:21:49.040
of the state of New
California and New Nevada.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:51.200
I was born and raised in old California.

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:53.800
So I was actually quite
surprised to learn of the 51st

00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:57.780
and 52nd state of New
California and New Nevada.

00:21:57.780 --> 00:21:59.900
Not surprisingly, this legal brief

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:04.674
is sovereign citizen level,
absolute garbage nonsense.

00:22:04.674 --> 00:22:06.800
We're all dumber for having Reddit,

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:09.140
and may God have mercy on their soul

00:22:09.140 --> 00:22:12.970
for actually filing this
nonsense amicus brief.

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:14.783
- May God have mercy on your soul.

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:17.750
- Hey, it's Devin from the future again.

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:20.640
And as I promised at the
beginning of this video,

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:24.120
we now have the known conclusion
to this particular lawsuit,

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:25.650
which is that the Supreme Court

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:27.940
has summarily dismissed the suit.

00:22:27.940 --> 00:22:31.130
And it did so in two
very terse paragraphs.

00:22:31.130 --> 00:22:33.120
The first stating that
the state of Texas's

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.120
motion for leave to
file a bill of complaint

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:36.420
is denied for lack of standing

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:38.240
under article three of the Constitution.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:39.970
Article three of the
Constitution is the one

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:43.360
that just says what jurisdiction
the courts actually have.

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:46.070
It creates the federal
courts in the United States.

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:47.970
And then it says, Texas
is not demonstrated

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:50.090
to judicially cognizable
interest in the manner

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:52.430
in which another state
conducts it elections.

00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:55.070
All of their pending motions
are dismissed as moot.

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:56.152
So there you have it.

00:22:56.152 --> 00:22:59.500
Texas doesn't have any cognizable injury.

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:01.430
It doesn't have standing to argue

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:04.510
that what other states do is bad

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:08.400
and that it can reverse the
results from different states.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:09.730
If it has an issue with the way

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:11.610
that things were run in its own state,

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:14.820
or if there are people who
were injured in those states,

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:16.350
then they can file suit

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:18.870
and they can sue those particular states.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:21.340
But the state of Texas has no interest

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:23.700
in being able to file a lawsuit,

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:25.890
no interest in the legal sense.

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:28.070
Obviously, we have an AG here

00:23:28.070 --> 00:23:30.660
who wants to overturn the
results, but in a legal sense,

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:34.190
they don't have an interest
to be able to file a lawsuit.

00:23:34.190 --> 00:23:36.470
It's like trying to make a lawsuit

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:38.450
over something that
happened to your neighbor

00:23:38.450 --> 00:23:41.670
when it didn't affect you
in any way, shape, or form.

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:42.960
And then, so a lot of people

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:45.850
get the second paragraph
of this dismissal wrong,

00:23:45.850 --> 00:23:48.350
which there are two justices that say

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:50.370
that they would have heard the case,

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:52.550
specifically statement of Justice Alito,

00:23:52.550 --> 00:23:54.177
with whom Justice Thomas joins.

00:23:54.177 --> 00:23:56.330
"In my view, we do not have discretion

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:58.040
to deny the filing of a bill of complaint

00:23:58.040 --> 00:24:00.530
in a case that falls within
our original jurisdiction.

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:02.920
See Arizona vs California citation.

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:04.410
I would therefore grant the motion to file

00:24:04.410 --> 00:24:06.800
the bill of complaint, but
would not grant other relief.

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:09.770
And I express no view on any other issue."

00:24:09.770 --> 00:24:12.540
Really all that saying is
that there are two justices

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:16.510
on the Supreme Court that
say that if a claimant,

00:24:16.510 --> 00:24:20.290
one of the states has a claim
under original jurisdiction,

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:21.810
the Court has to hear it.

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:24.010
And there are differing opinions on that.

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:26.230
Some, most of the justices
on the Supreme Court

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:27.660
say that it's discretionary

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:30.119
whether the Supreme Court
will take up the case.

00:24:30.119 --> 00:24:31.800
All these two people are saying,

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:34.220
Justice Alito and Justice
Thomas are saying is that,

00:24:34.220 --> 00:24:35.950
if there is a case that sounds

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:37.760
in the Court's original jurisdiction,

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:40.230
the Supreme Court of the
United States must hear it,

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:41.440
no exceptions whatsoever.

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:42.810
But almost certainly,

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:45.040
they wouldn't have granted the lawsuit.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.340
They wouldn't have granted the relief.

00:24:46.340 --> 00:24:48.230
They still would've kicked out the case.

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:49.910
They're just saying that they
would have heard the case,

00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:51.620
not summarily dismissed it.

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:53.810
And here's the thing about
this particular lawsuit.

00:24:53.810 --> 00:24:55.490
It was always going to fail.

00:24:55.490 --> 00:24:56.710
It was nonsense,

00:24:56.710 --> 00:24:58.760
whether the Supreme Court
actually heard it or not

00:24:58.760 --> 00:24:59.900
under original jurisdiction

00:24:59.900 --> 00:25:01.420
or just bounced it like they did,

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:02.740
it was always going to fail.

00:25:02.740 --> 00:25:03.720
And I'm torn on this.

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:05.900
Because you're allowed to file lawsuits,

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:07.270
even meritless ones,

00:25:07.270 --> 00:25:09.440
though there are mechanisms
for punishing people

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:11.610
